---
import Layout from '../layouts/BlogPost.astro';
---

<Layout
	title="Privacy"
	description="What this blog measures, what it deliberately doesn't, and how you can check."
	pubDate={new Date('July 14 2026')}
	updatedDate={new Date('Nov 21 2026')}
>
	<p>
		This site has no cookies, no trackers and no analytics script. The blog has no JavaScript at all;
		the Recouvrance pages have a few inline lines for their copy and pause buttons, which send
		nothing anywhere. Nothing is loaded from a third party, so nobody else sees that you were here.
	</p>

	<p>
		I do count readers, because I like knowing which posts are worth writing. But the counting
		happens on the server as it hands you the page — not in your browser. There is nothing to
		download, nothing to opt out of, and nothing to consent to.
	</p>

	<h2>What gets recorded</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>The path you visited, e.g. <code>/blog/linux_process/</code></li>
		<li>The <em>host</em> that linked you here, e.g. <code>news.ycombinator.com</code></li>
		<li>A country code, e.g. <code>FR</code></li>
		<li>Whether a page was opened, or a post was read to the end</li>
		<li>Whether the visit looked automated, and why: a bot user agent, or a datacenter network</li>
		<li>The time</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>What does not get recorded</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Your IP address</li>
		<li>Any cookie, visitor ID or fingerprint</li>
		<li>
			Your user agent and your network provider — they are looked at to spot bots, then dropped
		</li>
		<li>The full referring URL — only its host, so search terms never reach me</li>
		<li>Anything linking one page view to another, or one visit to the next</li>
	</ul>

	<p>
		There is no way for me to tell two readers apart, or to tell that you came back. That is
		deliberate: I get to see which posts land, and you stay anonymous.
	</p>

	<h2>"Read to the end"</h2>
	<p>
		A page view only says that a browser opened a page, and a good share of them are crawlers. So
		each post ends with an invisible 1×1 image, marked <code>loading="lazy"</code>: browsers only
		fetch it when you scroll close to it. That request is the read counter.
	</p>
	<p>
		Your browser keeps that image for a day, so reading the same post twice in a day counts once.
		That memory stays in your browser: the server never sees anything that tells readers apart.
	</p>

	<h2>Bots</h2>
	<p>
		Crawlers and headless browsers can look a lot like people. Before counting anything, the server
		checks two things it receives anyway: the user agent (does it say <code>bot</code>,
		<code>HeadlessChrome</code>, <code>curl</code>…?) and the network it comes from (a cloud
		provider like AWS or Google Cloud?). Only the verdict is kept. Commercial VPNs and privacy
		relays are not treated as bots.
	</p>

	<h2>Don't take my word for it</h2>
	<p>
		The blog is open source, and one file does all the counting:
		<a href="https://github.com/nahuel11500/my-blog-site/blob/main/worker/index.ts">
			worker/index.ts
		</a>.
		You can also open the network tab: at the end of a post you'll see one extra request,
		<code>/read/&lt;post&gt;/</code>, a 42-byte transparent image. That's all there is.
	</p>
</Layout>
